### 3.4 Multi-task learning with climate data

We also experiment with making our multi-task setup explicit by creating task-specific prediction heads for auxiliary labels, and adapt our loss function according to [Equation 4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.05845v6#S3.E4 "In 3.4 Multi-task learning with climate data ‣ 3 Predicting image geolocations ‣ PIGEON: Predicting Image Geolocations"), where ℒn,locsubscriptℒ 𝑛loc\mathcal{L}\_{n,\text{loc}} corresponds to the loss in [Equation 3](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.05845v6#S3.E3 "In 3.2 Hierarchical image geolocalization using distance-based label smoothing ‣ 3 Predicting image geolocations ‣ PIGEON: Predicting Image Geolocations"). Our multi-task setup further includes a cross-entropy classification task (ℒn,climatesubscriptℒ 𝑛climate\mathcal{L}\_{n,\text{climate}}) of the 28 different Köppen-Geiger climate zones Beck et al. ([2018](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.05845v6#bib.bib4)), a cross-entropy month (season) classification task (ℒn,monthsubscriptℒ 𝑛month\mathcal{L}\_{n,\text{month}}), and six mean squared error (MSE) regression tasks (combined into ℒn,regsubscriptℒ 𝑛reg\mathcal{L}\_{n,\text{reg}}) that attempt to predict values related to the temperature, precipitation, elevation, and population density of a given location.

