We unfreeze the last CLIP layer to allow for parameter sharing across tasks with the goal of observing a positive transfer from our auxiliary tasks to our geolocalization problem and to learn more general image representations reducing the risk of overfitting to the training dataset. Adjusting α,β 𝛼𝛽\alpha,\beta, and γ𝛾\gamma, our loss function weighs the geolocalization task as much as all auxiliary tasks combined considering each task’s loss magnitude. A novel contribution of our work is that we use a total of eight auxiliary prediction tasks instead of just two compared to prior research Pramanick et al. ([2022](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2307.05845v6#bib.bib26)).

